---
/**
 * /why-directhired — the brand and trust page (core-pages design spec §4).
 *
 * Spec §3.2 gives it five parts and no others: origin · the three pillars
 * at length · `TwoSidedMatch` · credentials · CTA. It is the page a family
 * lands on from a name search, so its job is to say what DirectHired is
 * FOR and then hand over three facts that do not depend on our word.
 *
 * THE ORIGIN IS SOURCE BRIEF §35, VERBATIM, AND NOTHING EXPANDED.
 * Spec §3.4 records that as a decision taken by default, because
 * DirectHired did not supply their own words: the headline ("We don't just
 * fill vacancies."), the origin sentence and the answer sentence below it
 * are all §35 as written. There is no founder here, no year of insight and
 * no anecdote, because nobody has given us one — spec §9 still lists
 * "founding story in DirectHired's words" as an open input, and inventing
 * one is exactly what master brief §78 forbids. The homepage's Difference
 * block (04) carries a compressed retelling of the same story; this is the
 * canonical wording, and when DirectHired supplies their own, BOTH change.
 *
 * THE PILLARS ARE STATED AT LENGTH — from sources, not from adjectives.
 * Their names and their one-line forms live in src/sections/Difference.astro
 * and come from master brief §1. What each one gains here traces to
 * something already supplied:
 *
 *   Better Matching     — brief §1's list of what is understood before a
 *                         recommendation (the family, the household, the
 *                         requirements, the skills, the lifestyle), plus
 *                         §40's ban on search/filter/browse, which is a
 *                         CONSEQUENCE of that order and is stated here as
 *                         one for the first time.
 *   Transparent Pricing — what /pricing actually does: both packages
 *                         itemised, the one line that differs named, and
 *                         what sits outside a total stated in the same
 *                         place. No figure is repeated here; a total typed
 *                         into this page is where a stale number hides.
 *   Personalised Service— brief §1 line 61 ("human guidance rather than
 *                         treating customers as another vacancy/order")
 *                         and line 689 ("personalised human consultation").
 *
 * PERSONALISED SERVICE IS THE SHORT ONE, DELIBERATELY. Two sentences, and
 * that is the whole of what this pillar can carry. The INTAKE role is
 * supplied and is not the gap: brief line 353 (and §74/line 1856) says
 * requirements are received and reviewed by the sales consultant, with a
 * target response within 1 business day, and the FAQ publishes exactly that.
 * What is unconfirmed is narrower — whether the SAME person carries a
 * placement end to end. Difference.astro records the same finding, and §78
 * forbids inventing staff detail, so the sentence that would say so is not
 * written. That one question is in docs/OPEN-DECISIONS.md; it is not an
 * author's to answer.
 *
 * NO LOAN OR PLACEMENT FEE IS NAMED. Spec §2.4 requires copy that mentions
 * either to carry BOTH halves — the employer advances it and recovers it,
 * and it is ultimately the helper's cost — which a pillar body has no room
 * for without becoming an essay. Difference.astro reached the same
 * conclusion for the same reason. The pillar points at /pricing, which is
 * where a reader can be given both halves properly, and §2.5's compliance
 * gate is untouched.
 *
 * Section grounds run cream / white / deep / white / cream across the five
 * blocks (.why-origin, .why-pillars, TwoSidedMatch, .why-credentials,
 * FinalCta), registered and asserted in tests/section-rhythm.test.ts.
 *
 * WHY TwoSidedMatch IS RENDERED HERE. Spec §6 says block 07's
 * --color-deep remains the only palette REGISTER SHIFT, and this page has
 * exactly one: this section, the same component painting the same ground,
 * not a second dark surface invented for a second page. The rule is about a
 * page's ground rhythm containing at most one such shift, and spec §3.2
 * lists TwoSidedMatch in this page's composition by name. /find-your-helper
 * declined to render it, correctly — §3.2 does not list it there. That
 * reasoning does not reach this page.
 *
 * It also earns its place editorially: the Better Matching pillar above it
 * describes ONE side of a match, and this block is the site's only
 * statement that there are two. "Happy Employer. Happy Helper." appears
 * once per page, which is what brief §3's instruction against slogan
 * repetition asks for.
 *
 * WHY THE CREDENTIALS BLOCK IS NOT TrustBar. Spec §3.1 reuses TrustBar
 * unchanged, and the homepage's bar is the right shape for a sub-fold
 * strip: three facts, no explanation. This page's whole subject is whether
 * to believe the paragraphs above, so each credential needs the scope it
 * was measured over said out loud — which the bar has no room for and
 * should not grow. Every VALUE still comes from src/data/company.ts and
 * none is retyped, so the two surfaces cannot disagree.
 *
 * Deliberately NO MOM crest, seal or other government mark, here or
 * anywhere (open decision D-7). Whether DirectHired may display one is a
 * permission nobody in this repository holds; a licence number set in type
 * is safe, an official-looking mark is not.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Container from '../components/Container.astro'
import Button from '../components/Button.astro'
import TwoSidedMatch from '../sections/TwoSidedMatch.astro'
import FinalCta from '../sections/FinalCta.astro'
import { company } from '../data/company'
import { defaultWhatsappUrl } from '../lib/whatsapp'

const whatsappHref = defaultWhatsappUrl()

const title = 'Why DirectHired — Understand First, Recommend Second'
/*
 * Task 8. The licence number was a LITERAL here — '23C1443' typed into the
 * meta description while the credentials block below read the same value
 * from company.ts. Two copies of a regulator-issued identifier, and the
 * copy in the <head> is the one nobody re-checks because nobody sees it
 * rendered. Master brief §70 forbids modifying or substituting this value,
 * and a second copy is how a modification happens by halves. Resolved from
 * the single definition, and guarded site-wide in tests/company.test.ts.
 */
const description =
  `Why families choose DirectHired: a licensed Singapore maid agency (MOM licence ${company.momLicence}) ` +
  'that understands your household before recommending a helper, with both packages itemised.'
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} breadcrumb="Why DirectHired">
  <section class="why-origin">
    <Container>
      {/*
        Source brief §35, all three lines, as written. The headline is its
        headline, the lede is its origin story and the sentence after it is
        its answer. Do not embroider any of the three: spec §3.4 records
        this as a default decision precisely because DirectHired has not
        supplied their own account, and an invented detail here would be
        the hardest kind to remove later — it would read as history.
      */}
      <h1>We don’t just fill vacancies.</h1>
      {/*
        SOURCED, NOT RETYPED (2026-08-17) — see src/data/company.ts's
        `foundingStory`. This and /about carried the mandated §3.4 sentence as
        two separate hardcoded literals, and Difference.astro carried a
        paraphrase of it. One string now feeds all three.
      */}
      <p class="origin-story">{company.foundingStory}</p>
      <p class="origin-answer">
        We take time to understand your needs before recommending a helper.
      </p>
    </Container>
  </section>

  <section class="why-pillars">
    <Container>
      {/*
        Structural as well as editorial: the three pillars below are <h3>s,
        so without this <h2> the document would run <h1> straight to <h3>
        and break its own outline. tests/pages.test.ts asserts it —
        heading-order is an axe BEST-PRACTICE rule and sits outside the
        wcag2a/wcag2aa tags scripts/run-axe.mjs filters to, so nothing else
        would catch it.
      */}
      <h2 class="section-title">What that looks like in practice</h2>

      <div class="pillars">
        {/*
          THE THREE PILLARS, AT LENGTH — master brief §1's names, and the
          same three the homepage carries in one line each. A visitor who
          has read the homepage must get something new here, so each body
          adds what its one-liner had no room for. Nothing below is a new
          CLAIM: every sentence restates something the brief supplied or
          something another page on this site already does.
        */}
        <div class="pillar">
          <h3>Better Matching</h3>
          {/*
            "in that order" — NOT "in that order, every time". Brief §1 says
            "Understand first. Recommend second."; "every time" turns a
            description of how DirectHired works into an absolute guarantee
            about every placement it has ever run, which is a promise nobody
            supplied and nobody here can check. The homepage states the same
            pillar without it (Difference.astro: "We understand first. We
            recommend second."), and this page should not be the one place
            that intensifies it.
          */}
          <p>
            Understand first, recommend second — in that order. We go through the
            household before we suggest anyone: the family, the routines, the tasks a helper would
            take on, the skills the job calls for, and what you expect day to day.
          </p>
          {/*
            The consequence, stated as one. Brief §40 rules out search,
            filtering and browse for the whole site, and a visitor who
            notices there is no gallery here reads its absence as a page
            that has not been built rather than as the product. Said
            plainly it is the argument: a shortlist made before the
            household is understood can only have been made from
            availability, which is what §35's origin sentence describes.
          */}
          <p>
            It is also why this site has no gallery of helpers to browse. A shortlist assembled
            before anyone has heard about your household is a shortlist assembled from whoever
            happens to be available — which is the thing DirectHired was started to stop doing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pillar">
          <h3>Transparent Pricing</h3>
          {/*
            NOT ONE FIGURE ON THIS PAGE, and that is not squeamishness: a
            total typed here would be a second copy of a number that lives
            in src/data/pricing.ts, which is exactly where a stale price
            hides. The claim is about the SHAPE of the price list, and the
            page that holds the figures is one link away.

            No completeness promise either. "Itemised" is what /pricing's
            own <h1> claims and all it may claim — the loan figure (§2.3)
            and the repayment mechanics (§2.5) are deliberately withheld,
            so "in full" is a promise this site is built not to keep.
          */}
          <p>
            Both packages are published on this site, itemised line by line, with the one line that
            differs between them named as well. You do not have to ask for a price list, and you are
            not handed a single total with the working folded up inside it.
          </p>
          <p>
            What sits outside a package total is set out in the same place as the totals themselves,
            on the <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a> — not left to come up later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pillar">
          <h3>Personalised Service</h3>
          {/*
            TWO SENTENCES, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF IT. See the file
            header: brief line 61 and line 689 are the supplied basis for
            this pillar, and the one thing a third sentence would naturally
            add — that the same person sees a placement through from the
            first review to the placement itself — is unconfirmed staff
            detail that §78 forbids inventing. (The intake role IS supplied,
            at line 353, and the FAQ publishes it.) A short honest pillar
            beats a long invented one.

            "A system", never "AI", "an algorithm" or "automated": there is
            no matching software, and the guard in tests/pages.test.ts
            correctly fires on any of those words near a matching verb even
            when they are being DENIED.

            ONE CLAUSE WAS DELETED HERE ON 2026-08-16, at DirectHired's own
            decision. It read: "…and you are not asked to put your family into
            a category before anyone has spoken to you." That is a claim about
            the LEGACY EMPLOYER-REQUIREMENT FORM, which spec §2.6.5 puts
            outside this project, which nobody in this repository has seen,
            and whose URL 404s today. The primary CTA on this page leads
            straight to it, so a helper-type or source dropdown on that form
            would falsify the sentence on the very next click. Asked, and
            DirectHired chose to delete it rather than defend it.

            It may be RESTORED, unchanged, if DirectHired confirms what fields
            that form actually asks for — specifically that it collects no
            helper-type, source or category selection before a conversation.
            Until then this pillar says only what this site can be held to.
          */}
          <p>
            Human guidance throughout the process, from your first questions through to the
            placement itself. A person reads what you send and answers you; nothing here is sorted
            by a system.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/*
        The internal linking triangle (spec §4) from its third vertex.
        /pricing is linked from the pillar above; this points at the page
        that shows the order those first questions actually run in.
      */}
      <p class="pillars-link">
        What happens after you send us your requirements is set out step by step on the
        <a href="/find-your-helper">find your helper</a> page.
      </p>

      {/*
        The page's first way to act, at the point the argument has just been
        made. Desktop has no sticky CTA bar, so without this a reader
        scrolls the whole page before meeting one. Primary before secondary,
        both from the single-sourced URLs, exactly as every other CTA
        surface on the site renders them.
      */}
      <div class="pillars-cta">
        <Button variant="primary" href={company.requirementFormUrl}>Submit Your Requirements</Button>
        <Button variant="secondary" href={whatsappHref} external>WhatsApp Us</Button>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </section>

  {/*
    Block 07, unchanged and unparameterised (spec §3.1). It is this page's
    ONE palette register shift, and the section directly above it has just
    described one side of a match — see the file header for why rendering
    it here is not the second dark band spec §6 warns about.
  */}
  <TwoSidedMatch />

  <section class="why-credentials">
    <Container>
      {/*
        THE HEADING AND THE LEDE BOTH OVERCLAIMED, and the page said so
        itself three rows later (2026-08-17).

        The heading was "What you can check" and the lede was "Everything
        above is a description of how we work. These three are not." — which
        asserts that all three values below are matters of record. They are
        not. The licence is: it is on MOM's public register. The founded year
        and the placement count are DirectHired's own, and a count of
        placements is registered nowhere at all. The licence note itself then
        called the licence "the one fact on this page that does not rest on
        our word", contradicting the lede two paragraphs above it.

        Meanwhile /about published the same values under "as the company
        gives them" — the opposite error, flattening the one value a family
        CAN check onto the same footing as the ones they cannot.

        The turn is still here and it is still what the page needs; what
        changed is that it now names both halves. Both pages open on this
        distinction and close on company.suppliedStanding, and both render
        company.licenceStanding on the licence row, so neither can drift from
        the other again.
      */}
      <h2 class="section-title">What you can check, and what rests on our word</h2>
      <p class="credentials-lede">
        Everything above is a description of how we work. Below is the record behind it.
      </p>

      {/*
        EVERY VALUE COMES FROM src/data/company.ts. Not one is retyped —
        master brief §70 for the licence (never invent, modify, guess or
        substitute it) and §68 for the count, which was revised down from
        "1,000+" once already and would half-land if a literal sat on a
        page. tests/company.test.ts enforces both across every surface.
      */}
      <dl class="credentials">
        <div class="credential">
          <dt>MOM licence</dt>
          <dd class="credential-value">{company.momLicence}</dd>
          {/*
            Verified against MOM's public directory before publication, as
            master brief §70 requires. What is published is the number and
            what it is — no crest, no seal, no claim of endorsement, and no
            "MOM-approved" (D-7). A number set in type is safe; a
            government mark is a permission nobody here holds.
          */}
          {/*
            Sourced, not retyped. This said "the one fact on THIS PAGE that
            does not rest on our word" — true of the block, false of the page,
            and it contradicted the lede that used to sit above it. The
            single-sourced sentence scopes the claim to the block ("the one
            value HERE") and renders identically on /about.
          */}
          <dd class="credential-note">{company.licenceStanding}</dd>
        </div>

        <div class="credential">
          <dt>Established</dt>
          <dd class="credential-value">Since {company.foundedYear}</dd>
          <dd class="credential-note">
            The year DirectHired began placing domestic helpers with families in Singapore.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="credential">
          <dt>Track record</dt>
          {/*
            "Placements", never "helpers placed". The figure's basis, on
            company.placementCount, is "500+ placements across all services
            since 2022" — DirectHired's own sentence, and their note says
            it explicitly includes transfers, replacements and direct-hire
            processing. "Helpers placed" reads as new placements only,
            which is a smaller and stronger claim than the number supports.
            The note below carries the scope because §68 requires a figure
            to be published with the period and scope it was measured over,
            and this page has the room the homepage's trust bar does not.
          */}
          <dd class="credential-value">{company.placementCount} placements</dd>
          <dd class="credential-note">
            Across all services since {company.foundedYear}: new placements, transfers, replacements
            and direct-hire processing. That is the scope the figure was counted over.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      {/*
        The same closing sentence /about carries, in the same position and
        from the same constant. It goes after the list rather than before it
        because it is a statement about the rows, and it cannot be read until
        the reader has seen which row is the exception.
      */}
      <p class="credentials-standing">{company.suppliedStanding}</p>
    </Container>
  </section>

  {/*
    The lede read "What we ask you first is the whole of the difference this
    page describes", which claimed more than the page argues: of the three
    pillars, only Better Matching is about the first conversation. Transparent
    Pricing is about what is published on /pricing, and Personalised Service
    is about the guidance running through the whole placement. "Where it
    starts" is the true version of the same invitation, and it still points at
    the one thing a family can test on day one — which is what the heading
    above asks them to do.
  */}
  <FinalCta
    heading="Judge it by the first conversation."
    lede="Send us what your family needs. The questions we ask first are where the difference this page describes starts."
  />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .why-origin {
    /*
     * --space-12 top, --space-section bottom — Hero's treatment, and
     * /pricing's and /find-your-helper's, for the same reason all three
     * use it: this block carries the page's hero, and a full
     * --space-section (128px at ≥1280) above an image-less single-column
     * headline spends the first screen on cream.
     */
    padding-block: var(--space-12) var(--space-section);
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }

  .why-origin h1 {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 16ch;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--size-h1);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .origin-story {
    max-width: 56ch;
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-lede);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  /* The answer half of brief §35, in full ink rather than muted: the
     paragraph above it is the complaint a visitor arrived with, and this
     is the sentence the rest of the page is an expansion of. */
  .origin-answer {
    max-width: 44ch;
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-lede);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .why-pillars {
    padding-block: var(--space-section);
    /* White, between the cream hero above and TwoSidedMatch's deep band
       below. Registered in tests/section-rhythm.test.ts; the grounds have
       to be judged as a sequence, so do not flip this one alone. */
    background: var(--color-surface-raised);
  }

  /* Shared by both page-local blocks' <h2>, at --size-h3 rather than
     --size-h2: these head a sub-part of a page the <h1> has already
     opened, and setting them at section-header scale would put three
     competing display sizes on one screen. Same size and role as
     /pricing's `.grid-heading` and /find-your-helper's `.section-title`. */
  .section-title {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--size-h3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /*
   * Each pillar is a ROW — title on its own column, body beside it —
   * rather than the homepage's three-abreast divided row.
   *
   * That is not variety for its own sake. Difference.astro's layout is
   * built for one sentence per pillar and works because all three are the
   * same short length; these bodies run to a paragraph or two, and three
   * of those side by side at 1280 give each ~340px and a wall of eleven
   * lines. A row per pillar gives the body a reading measure and lets the
   * three titles stack on one axis, which is what makes the page scan as
   * an argument in three parts rather than as three panels.
   */
  .pillars {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-8);
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
  }

  /*
   * Opened by a hairline in --color-border (decorative, ~1.3:1 — see
   * tokens.css; it carries no text and bounds no control), never
   * --color-brand-teal. Teal as a drawn line is reserved for exactly two
   * places on this site, block 07's crossbar and FinalCta's mark, and
   * spending it on routine section furniture is what makes those two stop
   * meaning anything.
   */
  .pillar {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    padding-top: var(--space-6);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .pillar h3 {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .pillar p {
    max-width: 56ch;
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  .pillars-link {
    max-width: 56ch;
    margin: var(--space-12) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  /* --color-accent (6.03:1 on cream, 5.83:1 on white), never
     --color-brand-teal, which measures 2.89:1 and carries no text
     anywhere on this site. */
  .pillar a,
  .pillars-link a {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-accent);
  }

  .pillar a:hover,
  .pillars-link a:hover {
    color: var(--color-accent-hover);
    border-color: var(--color-accent-hover);
  }

  .pillars-cta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
  }

  .why-credentials {
    padding-block: var(--space-section);
    /* White again, between TwoSidedMatch's deep band above and FinalCta's
       cream below. FinalCta is fixed at --color-surface because the
       homepage renders it too, which is what fixes this one. */
    background: var(--color-surface-raised);
  }

  .credentials-lede {
    max-width: 46ch;
    margin: var(--space-4) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-lede);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .credentials {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-8);
    margin: var(--space-12) 0 0;
  }

  .credential {
    padding-top: var(--space-6);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  /* The site's established eyebrow treatment (TrustBadge's .trust-label):
     small, semibold, uppercase, tracked. --tracking-wide's contract
     requires text-transform: uppercase in the same rule — asserted in
     tests/tokens.test.ts, which scans src/pages too. */
  .credential dt {
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-small);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  /*
   * The standing sentence under the list. Note size and muted ink — the same
   * treatment .credential-note gets, because it does for the block what a
   * note does for a row. It is not a second lede.
   */
  .credentials-standing {
    max-width: 46ch;
    margin: var(--space-8) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  /* Figtree, not Fraunces, and tabular figures — the same treatment
     TrustBadge gives these three values on the homepage, so a licence
     number reads as a number rather than as display copy. */
  .credential-value {
    margin: var(--space-2) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-h3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .credential-note {
    max-width: 42ch;
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  /* 48em = --bp-tablet (tokens.css); the literal is repeated by hand
     because custom properties do not resolve inside a media condition. */
  @media (min-width: 48em) {
    /* The title column is fixed rather than fluid so the three bodies
       start on one axis; `minmax(0, 1fr)` on the second track lets it
       shrink below its content's min-content width instead of pushing the
       row wider than the container. */
    .pillar {
      grid-template-columns: 16rem minmax(0, 1fr);
      column-gap: var(--space-8);
      align-items: baseline;
    }

    /*
     * Explicit placement, not auto-flow. A pillar with two paragraphs
     * auto-places them h3 / p in row 1 and the SECOND p back under the
     * title in row 1 of column 1 — the title column filling up with body
     * copy, which is the layout this row shape exists to avoid. Pinning
     * the title to the first cell and every paragraph to column 2 stacks
     * the bodies against each other whether a pillar has one or two.
     */
    .pillar h3 {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: 1;
    }

    .pillar p {
      grid-column: 2;
    }

    .credentials {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
